1.37.911.928 D Parsed message: {"role":"assistant","content":"### Difference Between a Process and a Thread\n\nTo understand the difference, it helps to visualize them as organizational units within an operating system.\n\n#### 1. Process\nA **process** is an instance of a computer program that is being executed. It is the fundamental unit of resource allocation.\n*   **Isolation:** Each process has its own private memory space (heap, stack, data segments). If one process crashes, it generally does not affect other processes.\n*   **Overhead:** Creating and switching between processes is expensive because the OS must manage separate memory maps, page tables, and context switches.\n*   **Communication:** Processes communicate via Inter-Process Communication (IPC) mechanisms like pipes, sockets, or shared memory, which are slower and more complex.\n\n#### 2. Thread\nA **thread** is the smallest unit of execution within a process. A process can have multiple threads.\n*   **Shared Resources:** Threads within the same process share the same memory space (heap, global variables, file descriptors). Each thread has its own stack and register state.\n*   **Lightweight:** Creating and switching between threads is much faster and consumes less memory than processes.\n*   **Communication:** Threads communicate easily by reading/w"}
