1.50.269.115 D Parsed message: {"role":"assistant","content":"Introducing a message queue (MQ) between two services is a classic architectural decision that shifts the system from **synchronous coupling** to **asynchronous decoupling**. Here’s a breakdown of the trade-offs:\n\n### ✅ What It Buys You (Benefits)\n\n1. **Decoupling**\n   - **Temporal Decoupling**: The producer doesn’t need the consumer to be available at the time of sending. If the consumer is down, messages are stored until it recovers.\n   - **Logical Decoupling**: Producers don’t need to know the identity, number, or implementation details of consumers. They just publish to a topic/queue.\n\n2. **Load Leveling (Peak Shaving)**\n   - Absorbs traffic spikes. Producers can send messages at high rates, while consumers process them at their own sustainable pace. This prevents downstream services from being overwhelmed.\n\n3. **Reliability & Durability**\n   - Messages are persisted (depending on MQ configuration). If a consumer crashes mid-processing, the message can be redelivered. This provides at-least-once delivery guarantees.\n\n4. **Scalability**\n   - Consumers can be scaled horizontally independently of producers. You can add more"}
